## 1. Retry all three checks as one block with `expect().toPass()`

Keep the save **outside** the retried block. Fetch the API response **inside** it so every attempt reads fresh state.

```ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('saves the profile', async ({ page }) => {
  const newName = 'New Picker';
  // Use your application's actual avatar URL format.
  const expectedAvatarSrc = `/api/avatar?name=${encodeURIComponent(newName)}`;

  await page.goto('/profile');
  await page.getByLabel('Name', { exact: true }).fill(newName);
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save', exact: true }).click();

  // Avoid nesting long locator retries inside the block's retry loop.
  const check = expect.configure({ timeout: 0 });

  await expect(async () => {
    await check(page.getByRole('status')).toContainText(newName);
    await check(page.getByTestId('profile-avatar'))
      .toHaveAttribute('src', expectedAvatarSrc);

    const response = await page.request.get('/api/profile');
    check(response.ok()).toBe(true);
    check(await response.json()).toMatchObject({ name: newName });
  }).toPass({
    timeout: 10_000,
    intervals: [100, 250, 500],
  });
});
```

`toPass()` needs an explicit timeout; its default is `0`, independent of the normal assertion timeout.

**Toast caveat:** this requires the toast to remain visible until the backend converges. If it disappears before then, “all three pass in the same attempt” is not a valid requirement—check the toast separately, then retry the persistent state.

## 2. Isolate the server-side account, not just the browser context

`storageState` isolates browser authentication state; it does **not** clone the account in the database. All 20 tests are racing to change the same language setting.

**Recommended: give each test its own picker account**, with a known initial language. That also isolates retries, workers, and shards.

Here is an exact fixture pattern. The `/test-support/*` routes below are **example application-owned test APIs**, not Playwright APIs. This example assumes account creation also sets the authentication cookie.

```ts
// fixtures.ts
import { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';
import { test as base, expect } from '@playwright/test';

export const test = base.extend({
  storageState: async ({ playwright, baseURL }, use) => {
    const api = await playwright.request.newContext({ baseURL });
    let accountId: string | undefined;

    try {
      // Contract: creates a picker, sets language, and logs this API context in.
      const created = await api.post('/test-support/pickers', {
        data: {
          username: `picker-${randomUUID()}`,
          language: 'en',
        },
      });
      expect(created.ok()).toBe(true);
      accountId = (await created.json()).id;

      // The test's browser context now authenticates as this unique account.
      await use(await api.storageState());
    } finally {
      try {
        if (accountId) {
          const deleted = await api.delete(
            `/test-support/pickers/${encodeURIComponent(accountId)}`,
          );
          expect(deleted.ok()).toBe(true);
        }
      } finally {
        await api.dispose();
      }
    }
  },
});

export { expect };
```

Import this fixture in every relevant spec:

```ts
import { test, expect } from './fixtures';

test('changes language', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/settings');
  await page.getByLabel('Language').selectOption('fr');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save', exact: true }).click();
  await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Paramètres' }))
    .toBeVisible();
});
```

A **per-worker account pool** also works, but account allocation must be unique across CI shards, and each test must restore a known language before starting.

**If only one account is available**, serialize every test that mutates it:

```ts
// playwright.config.ts
import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';

export default defineConfig({
  fullyParallel: false,
  workers: 1,
});
```

That only serializes **one Playwright invocation**. Do not run this shared-account group concurrently on multiple shards, projects, or CI jobs.

